WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.659 --> 00:00:06.109
The Dark Knight is a beacon of hope, literally,
in one of the most goosebump-inducing scenes

00:00:06.109 --> 00:00:09.559
from "The Batman" – and fans can't stop
talking about it.

00:00:09.559 --> 00:00:12.059
Warning: spoilers for "The Batman" ahead.

00:00:12.059 --> 00:00:18.310
"The Batman" is darker than any previous iteration
of the masked vigilante's story — with Variety

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:23.289
describing it as a "grounded, frequently brutal"
film noir.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:29.570
Total Film magazine quoted director Matt Reeves
as calling his movie the scariest "Batman"

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.410
movie yet, and critics and audiences seem
to agree.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:39.030
One element adding to the movie's dark tone
is the way Batman is treated by citizens of

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.030
Gotham.

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:42.110
At best, they're wary of him.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:47.120
Two years into his career as a crime-fighter,
he's not exactly seen as a superhero.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:51.469
Not even the Christopher Nolan movies made
the Batman seem like such a threat to regular

00:00:51.469 --> 00:00:52.609
people.

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:56.870
This is made clear at the start of the film
when on Halloween night a gang of thugs beat

00:00:56.870 --> 00:00:59.859
up on a man riding public transportation.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:04.700
When Batman swoops into a subway station to
mete out his personal brand of justice, he

00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:07.750
lays the bad guys out with ruthless efficiency.

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:13.570
But then, instead of being grateful, the victim
cowers and asks Batman not to hurt him.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:19.500
The scene establishes Batman as a frightening
force for everyone – not just the bad guys.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.240
That's the way it stays for most of the movie,
with comparisons being drawn between the supposedly

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.220
heroic Batman and the supposedly villainous
Riddler.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.960
But that all starts to change at the end of
the film with a scene that's giving fans goosebumps

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.570
— and for good reason.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.450
"Fear is a tool."

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.950
This goosebumps-inducing scene takes place
at the very end of the film.

00:01:41.950 --> 00:01:46.320
Batman has failed in his efforts to contain
the Riddler's damage, not realizing until

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.490
too late that the criminal has placed explosives
at seven of Gotham's sea walls.

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:57.360
When they go off, water floods the city — including
Gotham Square Garden, where the mayor-elect

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:03.360
Bella Reál is giving a victory speech to
a crowd full of Gotham's most important people.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:08.440
Batman soars in to save everyone, but before
he does that he has to deal with the internet

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.890
army that the Riddler has put together, who
are on hand to make sure the villain's plan

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:14.320
goes smoothly.

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:19.150
In the aftermath, a number of people are trapped
by the collapsed building, so Batman goes

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:20.360
to rescue them.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:25.140
At first, they are hesitant to accept Batman's
help, not knowing his motives.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:30.030
The first to take Batman's offered hand is
the young son of former mayor Don Mitchell

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.030
Jr.

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:35.250
Orphan Bruce Wayne sees a bit of himself in
the boy, whose father was killed at the start

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.250
of the film.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:38.500
A young woman tentatively follows.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.280
Eventually, everyone lets him lead them out
of there through the flooding waters.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:47.860
Batman ignites a red flare and holds it up
to light the way, becoming a literal beacon

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.830
of hope.

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:53.320
"I'm vengeance."

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:58.070
The scene is part of the character's own journey
in the film.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:02.840
Batman starts out moodily telling his enemies
that he's the personification of vengeance.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.930
Later, one of the Riddler's cronies says the
same about himself, when Batman asks him why

00:03:07.930 --> 00:03:10.710
he's helping to cause such terror in the city.

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:15.560
At that point, Batman realizes how much in
common he has with the bad guys, and it prompts

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.880
him to reflect on his mission, and what he
wants to stand for.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.710
At the end of the movie, Bruce realizes that
he can't just stand for vengeance – he has

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:25.460
to be a symbol of hope.

00:03:25.460 --> 00:03:30.380
This scene, coupled with Bruce's emotional
growth, allows the film to end on a somber

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.209
yet optimistic note.

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:36.600
Posters on Reddit discussed the scene in detail,
with one user on Reddit said praising the

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.650
hard-hitting theme.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.650
They wrote,

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:43.130
"This was a pivotal concept that Batman had
to confront in order to find a sense of justice

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.850
built on something more stable than anger
and vengeance.

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.800
Batman needs to be fear, but he also MUST
be hope."

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:52.920
One user on Reddit gave their thumbs-up as
well, writing,

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:57.840
"The whole character arc of 'The Batman' from
the vengeance to hope was showcased masterfully."

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.470
One user on Reddit thought the scene was meaningful
and visually beautiful, writing,

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:06.290
"That scene when he’s helping the people
in the flooded area and leading them to a

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:10.240
safe place is just beautiful, visually and
story wise.

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:13.099
Perfect depiction of Batman being the beacon
of hope."

00:04:13.099 --> 00:04:17.090
It makes sense that Batman would be facing
this character development at this early stage

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:18.570
of his career as a caped crime-fighter.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:24.040
Naturally, a city facing such rampant crime
would be suspicious of a masked interloper

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:27.090
diving in to take matters into his own hands.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.400
After dealing with the threat of the Riddler,
however, the people of Gotham – and Batman

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.470
himself – have a better idea of what he's
actually trying to accomplish.

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:36.470
"Justice.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.240
The answer's justice."

